We can add this however `$el` would not be a Cypress wrapped element, it would just be a jQuery wrapped element - which would make it easier to `cy.wrap`.

So this removes the boilerplate of manually `.each`ing each element and then needing to instantiate jquery instances. 

Also this example is contrived, you can already serially click a collection of elements with `.click({multiple: true})`. So where I see this being most valuable is conditional logic based on the `$el`.

``` javascript
cy.get("li")
  .each(function($el){
    // $el is wrapped jquery element
    if ($el.someMethod() === "something") {
      cy.wrap($el).click() 
    } else {
      // do something else
    }
  })
```

This is a little known fact but unique elements with an `id` are actually accessible as globals on `window`.

http://www.2ality.com/2012/08/ids-are-global.html

Cypress doesn't affect this behavior. What you should do is namespace your functions so they are not globals.

``` javscript
window.utils = {
  saveSegment: function(name, edit){  

  }
}

// call it
utils.saveSegment()
```

Another alternative is to add a build process around the cypress spec files so that you can use common js `require` or es6 `import` and not deal with globals at all.

